.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dch
keeps a program running in a detachable session (a fork of dtach). Run bare,
it attaches to the auto-named session for the current directory
(\fI<repo>\-<branch>\fR or the cwd basename), creating it if needed. The
session master survives terminal crashes, GUI quits, and SSH drops; a new
.B dch
in the same directory reattaches. While another client is attached, it
starts the next free
.IR <name>\-2 ,
.IR <name>\-3 ,
\&... instead;
.B \-f
or
.B \-n
attaches to the busy session. Detach with
.BR Ctrl\-\(rs .
.PP
If the socket node itself is deleted while the master is alive \(em some
external cleanup, not
.BR dch 's
own doing \(em the master notices on its next idle tick (hourly by default;
.B DCH_HEAL_MS
overrides it) and re\-binds the socket under the same name, re\-stamping the
version sidecar and refreshing the activity/state sidecars. Left alone, a
missing socket node is invisible to
.B \-\-status
and
.BR \-\-ls\-json ,
and the next attach starts a
.I second
master under the same name instead of finding the first. The heal never
touches a socket node that is present, so it cannot recover a
name a second master has already taken; see
.B DCH_SOCKET_DIR
below for avoiding the underlying cause.
.PP
Each session also keeps an in-memory terminal mirror (embedded
libghostty\-vt), which powers the control verbs below: scripts and coding
agents can type into, read, and wait on a session without attaching and
without disturbing an attached human.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
Pick a session interactively and attach; see
.B SESSION PICKER
for its tabs and keys.
When a session runs a named Claude Code or Codex session, that name is shown
as \fIdisplay-name (real-name)\fP. Claude names are read from
.IR ~/.claude/sessions ;
Codex titles from
.IR ~/.codex/session_index.jsonl .
Current Codex thread IDs come from matching shell-snapshot filenames; older
process-environment IDs remain supported. Both are joined only through a live
harness process's
.B DCH_SESSION
so stale records do not overlay; explicit aliases win.
.TP
.BR \-ls ", " \-\-ls
List sessions one per line as \fIdisplay-name (real-name)\fP when an alias or
harness title exists, otherwise as \fIreal-name\fP. Aliases win over harness
titles. Local only: no network, no
.B tailscale
call. With
.BR \-\-remote ,
then the remote hosts' sessions as \fIhost\fP:\fIname\fP (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" ),
a row of a host that did not answer this time ending in
.BR [stale] .
.TP
.B \-lj
List sessions as \fIname\fP<TAB>\fIalias\fP<TAB>\fIactivity_epoch\fP lines.
With
.BR \-\-remote ,
then the remote ones as \fIhost\fP:\fIname\fP<TAB>\fIalias\fP<TAB>0,
escaped.
.TP
.BI \-a " name"
Attach to a session; never create one. When no local session has exactly
that name and it has the form \fIhost\fP:\fIname\fP, attach to
.I name
on that remote host (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" );
an unknown host is an error that lists the known ones. With remote hosts
off
.RB ( DCH_NO_REMOTE=1 ,
.BR "remote_hosts = off" )
the name stays local.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " host"
With
.B \-a
or a remote control verb (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" ):
the session is on that remote host; its name may hold a colon.
With remote hosts off
.RB ( DCH_NO_REMOTE=1 ,
.BR "remote_hosts = off" )
it reaches only a host named in the same command's
.BR \-\-hosts ;
any other is
.IR "not a known host" ,
exit 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-cwd " dir"
With
.B \-\-spawn
only: the session's start directory. For a remote spawn it is a directory on
that host; for a local one it must exist here.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrollback " N"
With any attach
.RB ( \-a ,
.BR \-n ,
the picker): the attach replay first prints the last
.I N
lines of the session's scrollback, then the screen. They are ordinary output
scrolled off the top of the terminal's screen, so they land in the
terminal's own scrollback. A line is a mirror row at the window's width.
.I N
is capped at 10000 and at what the mirror keeps
.RB ( DCH_SCROLLBACK ),
and the whole replay at 2 MiB, past which the oldest lines go. 0, the
default, is the replay as before; this wins over
.BR DCH_REPLAY_SCROLLBACK .
The alternate screen has none to send. Ignored, silently, by a lite build,
.B DCH_NO_VT
or
.B DCH_NO_REPLAY
masters, and a master from before this option (the attach works as before).
With a remote host it is sent as the attach request's
.B scrollback
key (see
.BR "REMOTE SERVER" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " list"
The remote hosts, a comma-separated
.RI [ label =] host [: port ]
list (port 8771 when not given), instead of the
.B remote_hosts
key or tailnet discovery.
.TP
.B \-\-remote
With
.BR \-ls ,
.BR \-lj ,
.B \-\-ls\-json
or
.BR \-\-history :
the remote rows or records too (the picker always shows them).
.TP
.B \-\-ls\-json
List sessions as a JSON array of
.BR {name,alias,harness,activity_epoch,state,version,agents,meta,meta_epoch,auto_allowed,claude_session_id,transcript_path,cols,rows,clients,cwd} .
.I cwd
is last: the directory the session started in, from its live history
record, or
.B null
when it has none or the record says it ended.
.I cols
and
.I rows
are the pty size the session's master holds and
.I clients
how many clients are attached, read over the control socket without
attaching or resizing; all three are left out for a master too old to
report them, and for remote rows.
.I claude_session_id
is the sessionId of the one live interactive Claude Code under the session's
program, read fresh from its \fI<config>/sessions/<pid>.json\fP record (its
pid descends from the program and its procStart matches the process), or
.B null
when none runs there, more than one does, it has exited, or the id is not a
UUID.
.I transcript_path
is the absolute \fI<config>/projects/*/<sessionId>.jsonl\fP when exactly one
such file exists, else
.BR null .
.I auto_allowed
is the permission prompt the master answered last with auto\-allow (see
.BR "CONFIG FILE" ),
\fB{"at":\fP\fIepoch\fP\fB,"title":\fP\fItitle\fP\fB}\fP
(the title is the dialog's first line, e.g. "Bash command"), or
.BR null ;
it is cleared when the session's program exits.
The
.I harness
field carries the name of the Claude Code or Codex session running inside,
when one is; the real
.I name
and display
.I alias
remain separate fields.
With
.BR \-\-remote ,
the remote hosts' sessions follow as
.BR {name,alias,host,state,stale,cwd} :
.I name
and
.I alias
as the host sent them,
.I host
the host's label,
.I stale
true when the host did not answer this time and the row is its last answer,
.I cwd
the host's, or
.B null
from a server that does not send one.
.TP
.B \-rl
Rename (alias) sessions interactively. Aliases are display-only.
.TP
.BI \-m " name " [ alias ]
Set or clear an alias non-interactively. A name with no live session is
refused with
.BI "no session: " name
and exit 1, as
.B \-k
does.
.TP
.BR \-k " [\fIname\fP]"
Kill a session (interactive picker without a name).
.TP
.B \-kl
Kill all sessions.
.TP
.BR \-d " [\fIname\fP]"
Detach all clients of a session.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Override the auto-name.
.TP
.B \-f
Force-attach even if another client is connected.
.TP
.BI \-e " char"
Set the detach character (default
.BR ^\(rs ).
.TP
.B \-E
Disable the detach character.
.SH SESSION PICKER
.BR "dch \-l" ,
.B \-k
and
.B \-d
without a name, and the detach\-key switch open a full\-screen picker with
up to three tabs, each with its count:
.BR Sessions ;
.BR Sidekicks ,
the live sessions whose name, alias or harness title matches
.B sidekick_pattern
in the config file (a POSIX extended regular expression, case\-insensitive,
default
.BR \-qa ;
an invalid one, or one matching every name, is matched as plain text; empty
turns the tab off), shown only when there are some; and
.BR History ,
the ended sessions of
.BR \-\-history ,
one row per session name (its newest record, pinned when any record of
the name is): every pinned name, then the newest other names while there
are fewer than 50 rows, so pinned names alone can make more; with remote
hosts on, also the other hosts' (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" ),
as \fIhost\fP:\fIname\fP with the host faint, at most 50 names per host
on top of the local rows.
Rows sort pinned first (a remote row never is), then by the name shown
(alias, else agent name, else name; a remote row's starts with
\fIhost\fP:, and it sorts among the local ones), case\-insensitive,
digit runs by value.
.BR Enter ,
.B m
and
.B o
on one run
.B \-\-revive
there, which needs
.B dch serve \-\-allow\-create
on the host (else its one\-line refusal shows);
.B p
and
.B d
are for this host's records.
Every list has column headers and aligned columns sized to the rows on
screen.
A session row shows NAME, STATE as a glyph and a word (Needs input, Working,
Done, Idle), ACTIVE (how long ago it last printed: now, 22m, 3h, 2d), AGENTS,
MERGED and PULL REQUESTS (from
.BR \-\-meta 's
.BR prs :
.IR repo#N ,
the repo once when they share it, +N for those that do not fit).
A History row shows a pin mark, NAME, ENDED, WHEN and the DIRECTORY.
As the window narrows the last column goes first, the name shrinks last, and
nothing wraps.
The selection is a tinted bar the width of the screen, each cell keeping its
own colour; the key hints at the bottom are grouped (actions, the agents',
the view, moving about) and a narrow window drops whole groups.
The list reloads about once a second and keeps the selection on the same
session.
.PP
Keys: arrows or
.BR j / k ,
.BR PgUp / PgDn ,
.BR g / G
move;
.BR Tab ,
.BR \(-> / \(<- ,
.BR l / h
and
.B Shift\-Tab
switch tabs;
.B ,
opens the settings view;
.BR q ,
.B Esc
or
.B Ctrl\-C
quit.
.B /
filters, as in vim: a
.B /
prompt on the status line narrows every tab to the rows whose shown name,
dch name or directory contains the text typed (case\-insensitive), the tab
counts following, the selection on the top match, \(lqNo match\(rq when
none does.
While typing, printable keys go to the filter
.RB ( j
and
.B k
too),
.B Tab
switches tabs,
.B Backspace
edits (on an empty filter it closes the prompt),
.B Ctrl\-U
clears the text, the
.B Up
and
.B Down
arrows close the prompt and move, keeping the filter, so the letter actions
work on the filtered rows, and
.B Enter
closes the prompt and acts on the selected row as it always does;
.B /
reopens the prompt on the kept text.
The filter stays across tab switches and reloads until
.BR Esc ,
which clears it; a later
.B Esc
quits.
On Sessions and Sidekicks:
.B Enter
attaches (or kills, detaches for
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-d ),
.B r
renames,
.B R
restarts the agent
.RB ( "\-\-restart \-\-include\-agents \-f" ),
.B S
stops,
.B x
kills,
.B a
allows a plain Yes/No permission prompt (presses 1, Yes; any other prompt is refused),
.B b
restarts stale agents,
.B B
restarts all agents (force),
.B s
toggles the sort between activity (blocked, working, done, idle, newest
first) and name (numeric\-aware), saved as
.B picker_sort
for the next picker.
On History:
.B Enter
resumes
.RB ( \-\-revive ),
.B Shift\-Enter
or
.B o
resumes and then attaches to the session the revive made, once it is up
(a failed revive stays in the picker with its line; only in the pickers
that attach,
.B dch \-l
and the switch: the
.B \-k
and
.B \-d
pickers say so and do nothing),
.B m
resumes without the message,
.B I
resumes every interrupted session
.RB ( "\-\-revive \-\-all" ),
.B p
pins or unpins,
.B d
removes
.RB ( \-\-forget ),
every record of the row's name; while a session of that name is live, at
most 9 of its ended records per press (the rest stay a row: press
.B d
again).
Every action but attach, rename, resume and pin asks first and runs on
.BR y .
The status line shows the action while it runs, then its result.
Colour is truecolor when
.B COLORTERM
is truecolor or 24bit, else 256 colours for a 256color
.BR TERM ,
else the basic eight (the selection in reverse video);
.B COLORFGBG
naming a light background picks lighter tints.
.B NO_COLOR
draws no colour,
.B TERM=dumb
no colour and ASCII markers; without a terminal the session list is printed
instead.
While it is up the picker pushes the kitty keyboard protocol's disambiguate
flag
.RB ( "CSI > 1 u" ),
popped before it exits or attaches, so Ghostty, kitty and the like send
.B Shift\-Enter
apart from
.BR Enter ;
xterm's modifyOtherKeys form is read too.
.B o
does the same on any terminal.
On Sessions and Sidekicks
.B Shift\-Enter
attaches, as
.B Enter
does.
.PP
A session the picker opened
.RB ( "dch \-l" ,
the switch, History's
.BR Shift\-Enter )
that ends (its program exits: Claude Code after
.B Ctrl\-D
twice, a shell's
.BR exit )
returns to the picker instead of the shell, on History with the record
that end wrote selected;
.B q
there leaves for the shell; so does
.BR Esc ,
but a filter kept from finding the row takes the first
.B Esc
and the second leaves.
A session attached by name
.RB ( "dch <name>" ,
.BR "dch \-a <name>" )
or a remote one still exits to the shell when it ends.
.PP
The settings view lists every config file key (see
.BR "CONFIG FILE" )
with its value, marked (default) when unset, and a line on what it is for.
.B Enter
toggles a boolean (turning
.B auto_allow
on asks first), steps a choice key to its next value, or reads a string on
the status line
.RB ( Ctrl\-D
keeps it);
.B u
resets the key to its default;
.B Esc
or
.B q
goes back. A change is written as by
.B dch \-\-config
and applies at once.
.SH SWITCHING SESSIONS
Pressing the detach character
.B twice
within the double\-press window detaches and opens the same interactive picker as
.BR "dch \-l" ,
so you can move straight into another session.
It opens where the last picker of that dch left off (its tab, sort and
.B /
filter) with the session just left selected, on whichever tab holds it now;
the first switch after a plain attach opens on that session too.
Quitting the picker leaves you
detached. A single press still detaches; it simply waits out the window first.
.PP
.B DCH_DOUBLE_TAP_MS
sets that window in milliseconds (default
.BR 300 ).
Setting it to
.B 0
disables switching and makes detach instant again.
.PP
With
.B detach_key = switch
in the config file, read at each attach, the two swap: one press opens the
picker and two detach. With the window at
.B 0
one press opens the picker at once and the key never detaches; quit the
picker or use
.BR "dch \-d" .
.SH FOCUS AND SIZE
A session has one size, and several clients can be attached to it. The size
belongs to the client whose window has focus. An attaching client turns on
focus reporting (DEC mode 1004) in its terminal. On a focus\-in report
.RB ( "ESC [ I" )
it sends its window size to the master again, even when the size is
unchanged, so the session takes that window's size back from another client.
.PP
The client passes focus reports to the program in the session only while that
program has mode 1004 set itself; otherwise it drops them. The client tracks
the mode in the session's output and gets it from the master's mode re\-arm
on attach. All other input passes through unchanged. A read that ends in a bare
ESC waits up to 50 ms for the rest of a report, which delays a lone Esc key by
up to that much. A key that follows the ESC inside the wait reaches the
program after the ESC, with the same gap it had, so the two are not read as an
Alt\-key; an Alt\-key the terminal sends in one write stays one.
.PP
.B DCH_FOCUS_RESIZE=0
(or
.BR false ", " no ", " off )
or
.B focus_resize = false
in the config file turns this off: the client neither enables focus
reporting nor intercepts any input.
.SH CONTROL VERBS
All verbs take the session
.I name
first, never attach, and are safe to run while a human is attached.
.TP
.BI \-\-spawn " name" " " [ \-\-size " CxR" ] " " [ cmd... ]
Start a headless session running
.I cmd
(default:
.BR $SHELL ).
Size defaults to
.BR $COLUMNS x $LINES ,
then 80x24. Prints the session name. Exits 1 if the session exists or
.I cmd
cannot be executed (the error is printed); a
.I cmd
that runs and exits at once is still a successful spawn. Like
.B \-\-revive
and a plain
.BR "dch <new>" ,
it sets
.B LC_TERMINAL=ghostty
unless the caller has it: Claude Code then keeps its statusline's OSC 8
links under
.B TERM=xterm\-256color
with no
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
and Node CLIs (supports\-hyperlinks), which never read it, keep OSC 8 out
of pipes and files. ssh sends it on (macOS's default
.BR "SendEnv LC_*" ).
It reaches every program in the session, not only Claude Code: a tool that
keys on
.B ghostty
may assume Ghostty\-only features the attaching terminal lacks.
dch sets no
.BR FORCE_HYPERLINK ;
a caller's own value reaches the session.
.TP
.BI \-\-xray " name " "" \fR[\fB\-\-clear\fR|\fB\-\-follow\fR]\fP " \-\-json"
X\-ray: print one JSON object
.RB ( schema:1 )
about session
.IR name :
its corpus (\fBclaude\fR, \fBshell\fR or \fBnone\fR, with a
.BR reason :
.BR ended ,
.B lite
or
.BR off )
and its chapters, one per prompt, oldest first, at most 5000.
Each chapter has an opaque
.BR anchor ,
an
.BR offset ,
.BR at ,
a
.B title
(at most 120 Unicode scalars),
.B kind
.RB ( prompt ,
.B command
or
.BR compact ),
.B partial
and
.BR error .
.B refs
lists the issues, pull requests, tickets, commits, files and URLs the corpus
mentions (at most 2000 keys), each with
.BR key ,
.BR count ,
.BR defined ,
.B title
and
.BR underline .
With
.BI \-\-ref " ref"
it streams that one ref's lines as NDJSON instead
.RB ( first ,
.BR definition ,
.BR mention ,
.BR tally ,
.BR progress ,
then
.BR done ),
light by default (stops at the first result or after
.B \-\-budget\-ms
.IR ms ,
50 to 300, default 300),
.B \-\-full
to read it all (a
.B \-\-budget\-ms
with it is accepted and has no effect; a file gives its first
.B mention
line and its newest 199, the rest counted in the tally's
.BR mentions_dropped ;
one
.B definition
line per title, at most 8; output capped at 4 MiB, then
.BR why:\(dqoutput\(dq );
.B \-\-scope session
is required (\fBproject\fR, also the default with no
.BR \-\-scope ,
and \fBall\fR come later and exit 2).
A reader that closes the pipe ends any form at dch's next write (at most
250 ms later for
.BR \-\-ref ),
silently, with exit 0 and no
.BR done ;
any other write failure exits 3.
Must be the first argument; every other flag exits 2.
.I name
is an agent UUID (the live session whose Claude Code has it, else the
newest ended History record carrying it), a History record name
.I file\fB.json\fP
(that ended record, even when a new session took its name, from History
only: a plain shell's
.I name\fB.json\fP
after its name starts again answers corpus
.B none
with reason
.BR ended ,
never the new ring),
or a session name
(the live session, else its newest ended record).
A
.I name
with a
.B :
is resolved locally first; only when nothing local matches and its host is
known (\fB\-\-hosts\fR, \fBremote_hosts\fR, a cached remote row) is it a
remote
.I host\fB:\fIname
(exit 2, not supported yet).
A Claude Code session's chapters come from its transcript, found by one
directory listing (a symlinked project directory is not followed; two hits
answer corpus
.B none
with the cwd's
.BR project )
and opened with
.BR O_NOFOLLOW ;
it is read as its first 16 MiB and its newest 256 MiB, and past that, when
the file shrinks during the read, or past 5000 chapters,
.B complete
is false with
.BR why:\(dqrange\(dq .
A shell's chapters come from its ring's OSC 133 marks: one per typed command
block (\fBA\fR, \fBB\fR, client input, \fBC\fR, a non-blank command),
once its rows scroll off the screen (commands still on it have none yet;
commands typed ahead in one burst give only the first one a chapter, the
others having had no client input inside their own B..C).
A query also reads the current primary screen after the ring: its rows count
as output (refs only, no chapter, no definition), and
.B lines
counts the rows read; while the alternate screen is active, or from a master
started by an older dch (until
.BR \-\-restart ),
the answer is the ring's alone, with no error and no
.BR why .
In a shell's ring,
evicted rows (the master's own status bit, reset by a clear; a dch 1.43.0
master has none, and there only a gap, a lost range or a cut first row
counts) or a gap make
.B complete
false with
.BR why:\(dqrange\(dq .
The ring is read 1 MiB at a time, so a query stays under 16 MB.
The object is capped at 4 MiB (refs go first, each counted in
.BR dropped_keys ,
then the oldest chapters;
.BR why:\(dqoutput\(dq ).
Nothing is written to disk and nothing is shelled out.
With
.BR \-\-clear ,
empty a live shell session's ring through its control socket (nothing is
typed into the session) and print
.BR {\(dqschema\(dq:1,\(dqsession\(dq,\(dqsession_id\(dq,\(dqcorpus\(dq,\(dqring_cleared\(dq} ;
.I name
is first matched as a live ring's exact session_id (\fBm\fR + 16 hex;
each master is asked for its ring's header only), then as a name.
A Claude Code session has no ring
.RB ( ring_cleared:false ),
nor has one with
.B xray_ring 0
or a ring paused for an agent: nothing is cleared there, and the screen
and scrollback stay.
Otherwise the clear blanks the mirror too, as \[u2318]K does (ED 2 and ED 3
into the mirror, never into the pty):
.BR \-\-read ,
.B \-\-read \-\-recent
and the next attach's replay show an empty screen and no scrollback, while
a client attached at that moment keeps the old screen until the program
repaints.
With
.BR \-\-follow ,
stream NDJSON: a
.B snapshot
line holding the object, then deltas as the corpus grows, at most one batch
per 250 ms:
.B chapter
(new, or a known anchor replaced),
.B ref
(the whole object of a changed key;
.B count:0
removes it, and for a shell
.B count
can fall and
.B underline
turn off),
.B evict
.RB ( seq ,
a shell ring's new head, first in its batch: drop the chapters below it),
and
.B reset
.RB ( why :
.B rotated
for a new transcript, a /clear, or a shell ring paused, resumed or
given a new session_id;
.B shrunk
for a cut transcript;
.B cleared
for a shell's clear, ED 3 or RIS, never with evict;
.B evicted
past 256 keys in one eviction, at most once a second;
.B full
for the whole-file index after a capped transcript), each followed by a
new snapshot.
The snapshot plus its deltas is the
.B \-\-json
answer of that moment; top\-level fields change only with a snapshot.
Past 5000 chapters, a Claude Code stream sends no line for the oldest
chapter
.B \-\-json
drops, so a client can hold more chapters than
.B \-\-json
shows.
No timer: a transcript is watched with kqueue (inotify on Linux), its
project directory for a new file, a shell through the master's wake (its
ring or its screen changed, an alternate\-screen switch included).
At most 4 shell followers per master: a fifth exits 1
.IR busy .
An ended session or a History record writes its snapshot and exits 0;
a live one exits 0 when the session ends, its connection drops, or the
reader closes the pipe, idle or not.
.B \-\-follow
takes no
.B \-\-ref
or
.B \-\-clear
(exit 2).
Exits 1
.I "no such session"
(also for an ended session or a record with
.BR \-\-clear ,
and when a master hangs up mid\-request, in any form;
.B \-\-ref
then keeps the lines already written and writes no
.IR done ),
.I "ambiguous session"
or, for
.B \-\-clear
in dch\-lite,
.IR "no x\-ray corpus" ;
2 bad usage or a bad or remote
.IR name ;
3 the session or its transcript could not be read (a transcript that is
there but cannot be opened counts).
No new session or rename takes a name of exactly
.B m
+ 16 hex digits in any case (a session_id; on a case\-insensitive file
system the name's socket answers to the id too):
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-\-spawn ,
.BR "\-\-revive \-\-as" ,
.BR \-m ,
the pickers' rename and
.BR "dch serve" 's
create and rename refuse it (also with surrounding whitespace) with exit 2 and
.IR "name looks like a session_id" .
A session that already has such a name keeps it;
.B \-n
and
.B \-\-xray
reach it by its exact listed name only:
.B \-n
refuses a case variant of it as a new name, and
.B \-\-xray
reads a case variant of it as an id.
Other verbs
.RB ( \-a ,
.BR \-\-read ,
.BR \-\-status ,
.BR \-\-send ,
.BR \-k )
reach it by any case on a case\-insensitive file system, like any name.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " name text..."
Type
.I text
(arguments joined with spaces) into the session, no enter.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " name text..."
Same as
.B \-\-send
plus a carriage return. Into a session running Claude Code (as its command or
typed into its shell) the text goes through the
.B \-\-say
composer gate instead: typed only over an empty composer, Enter pressed once it
shows and again while it stays. A failure exits 1 with
.RI "dch: " name ": " reason
.RB ( prompt ,
.BR draft ,
.BR not_typed ,
.BR not_submitted ,
.BR no_screen ,
or on a signal
.BR interrupted ", " withdrawn ", " draft_left )
on stderr. Other programs, masters without a mirror, dch-lite and your own
session (\fB$DCH_SESSION\fR) keep the plain burst.
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " name key..."
Encode and send keys mode-aware (kitty keyboard protocol, application cursor
keys) using the mirror's live terminal state. Key names:
.BR enter ", " esc ", " tab ", " space ", " backspace ", " delete ,
.BR up / down / left / right ", " home ", " end ", " pgup ", " pgdn ,
.BR f1 \(en f12 ,
single characters, with
.BR ctrl+ ", " alt+ ", " shift+
prefixes (e.g.
.BR ctrl+c ", " shift+tab ).
.TP
.BI \-\-read " name" " " [ \-\-ansi ] " " [ \-\-cursor ] " " [ \-\-recent " [\fIN\fP]]"
Print the session's rendered screen to stdout.
.B \-\-ansi
keeps colors/attributes and OSC 8 hyperlinks (each line opens and closes its
own) \(em including the faint attribute and the exact
foreground color, which is the only thing that separates a TUI's own ghost
text (placeholders, argument hints) from characters the human typed. The plain
dump drops that, so a watcher deciding whether the prompt box holds an unsent
message must read with
.B \-\-ansi
.RB and " \-\-cursor" ;
see
.I docs/ghost\-text.md
in the source tree.
.B \-\-recent
prints the last
.I N
lines including scrollback (default 100).
.B \-\-cursor
additionally prints
.RI \(lqcursor " row col visible wrap" \(rq
to
.IR stderr ,
taken from the same snapshot as the screen on stdout, so a caller painting
that screen knows where the caret is instead of inferring it.
.I row
and
.I col
are 1\-based like
.BR CUP ,
with the row counted from the top of the visible screen;
.I visible
is DEC mode 25 (0 = hidden);
.I wrap
is 1 when
.I col
is the last column and the
.I next
printed character soft\-wraps to the next row \(em the caret is still on
.IR col ,
not past it.
.I row
counts screen rows while the screen dump has its trailing blank lines
trimmed, so a caret parked below the last line of content reports a
.I row
past the end of the dump; leading and interior blank rows are kept, so row
.I N
is line
.I N
of the dump whenever the dump reaches that far. Not valid with
.BR \-\-recent
(the caret belongs to the visible screen, not the scrollback tail). Exit 0 on
success, 3 if the session has no mirror, 1 if
.B \-\-cursor
was asked of a session master that predates it (restart the session) or of a
screen so large the response was truncated (the caret would land on the wrong
row of the kept tail, so it is withheld).
.TP
.BI \-\-follow " name"
Stream the session's screen to stdout, read\-only, as self\-delimited
frames: one at once, then one whenever the screen, its size or the caret
changed, at most one per
.B DCH_FOLLOW_MS
(default 150 ms), until the session ends or the reader closes the pipe. A
frame is a one\-line ASCII header, then exactly
.I len
payload bytes:
.RS
.PP
.RI "dch\-frame " "len rows" x "cols crow ccol cvis" \(rsn
.PP
Fields are separated by single spaces.
.B dch\-frame
names this format; a future incompatible one gets a new first token.
.I len
is the payload byte count; the payload is byte\-for\-byte what
.B \-\-read
.I name
.B \-\-ansi
prints, its trailing newline included.
.IR rows x cols
is the screen size (the mirror grid, at most 1024x1024).
.I crow
and
.I ccol
are the caret, 1\-based like
.BR \-\-cursor ,
or
.B 0 0
when a screen over 2 MB was tail\-cut and the caret has no row in it;
.I cvis
is 1 visible, 0 hidden. Every frame is whole: one sent only because the
caret moved or was hidden still carries the size and the full payload.
.RE
.IP
A frame is sent only when it differs from the last one sent. An idle
session costs no rendering: the master answers \(lqunchanged\(rq. Like
.BR \-\-read ,
it never attaches, writes nothing to the session, sends no size, and leaves
the activity stamp and attached clients alone; a
.B \-\-restart
is followed through. Exit 0 when the session ends or the reader closes the
pipe (quietly), 1 if there is no such session, its master stops answering
or predates
.B \-\-follow
(restart the session), 3 if the session has no mirror.
.TP
.BI \-\-wait " name " \-\-match " str" " " [ \-\-timeout " ms" ]
Block until the rendered screen or recent scrollback contains the literal
substring
.I str
(at most 512 bytes); print the matching line. Default timeout 10000 ms.
Exit 0 on match, 2 on timeout, 3 if no mirror.
.TP
.BI \-\-restart " name" "\fR|\fP" \-\-all " " [ \-\-include\-agents ] " " [ \-f ] " " [ \-\-json ]
Re\-execute the session's master process onto the current
.B dch
binary, in place. The program inside the session keeps running: its process,
its pty, its screen and every attached client survive, because the master
replaces its own image with
.BR execv (2)
without forking, carrying its file descriptors across. The target is the
.B dch
binary that ran this command, so a session started from a versioned install
prefix still lands on the newly installed build. Use this after upgrading
.B dch
so long\-lived sessions gain new features without being recreated.
.B \-\-all
restarts every session whose reported version differs from this binary's;
.B \-f
restarts them all regardless. Exit 0 when the new image is serving, 1 if the
re\-exec failed (the old master rolls back and keeps serving) or if the master
is too old to know the verb, in which case the session must be recreated.
Scrollback held only in the mirror is not carried across a restart; the
visible screen is.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-include\-agents ,
the master pass is followed by restarting Claude Code agents: in every session
with
.BR \-\-all ,
in the named session only otherwise. Without it no agent is touched,
.B \-\-all
included.
.B \-\-include\-agents
without
.B \-\-restart
is a usage error. A session qualifies when a live interactive Claude session
record (under
.IR ~/.claude/sessions ,
or
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ,
as each process in the session's tree has them in its own environment)
belongs to a descendant of the session's child, with a matching process start
time, and Claude is its command (a
.B claude
token, directly or behind a wrapper such as
.BR "headroom wrap claude" ,
with the session's working directory). An agent
is restarted when its record's version differs from what that
.B claude
reports for
.B \-\-version
on the master's
.BR PATH ,
or always with
.BR \-f .
Each such agent is sent the pause message, typed only while its composer is
on screen and empty; Enter follows only once the text (or Claude's
.B [Pasted text #N]
placeholder) shows in the composer row, and the same holds for
.B /exit
and the resume message. All agents are paused first and then waited on
together. An agent is ready when its record reads idle, it has visibly taken
the pause message, no subagents show on its agent panel, and that holds for a
settle window. A record that has read
.B shell
(a background shell is running) for
.B DCH_AGENT_SHELL_MS
after the pause counts as idle. dch then holds the session, types
.BR /exit ,
answers
.B 1
(exit and stop tasks) if Claude Code asks about background work, and
names what that stopped in the result line and in the resume message. An
.B /exit
that does not end Claude is taken back out of the composer. dch
takes the session id from the last
.B claude \-\-resume
.I id
line Claude prints, and relaunches the original command with
.B \-\-resume
.I id
inserted after the
.B claude
token. Any
.BR \-\-resume ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-\-continue ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-session\-id
and
.B \-\-fork\-session
in the original are dropped, and so is Claude's one\-shot prompt (see
.BR \-\-revive ).
Once the new Claude is idle, dch types the
resume message and checks the new version. Each agent gets one result line,
and the exit status is 1 if any of them failed. A failed agent is left
running; one that crashed on
.B /exit
is not relaunched; its last output lines go into the report. Skipped: Codex
sessions, a
.B claude
typed into a session's shell
.RB ( in_shell :
.B /exit
would hand the shell back to run whatever followed
.B claude
on its line), a session with a pending respawn, and the session running the
command. A positional prompt argument is dropped on resume (see
.BR \-\-revive );
after an option dch does not know, followed by a word, the whole command is
kept and the prompt is sent again. A dch\-lite client skips this step, with a note when a
session runs claude and
.B \-\-include\-agents
was given. The step can take up to
.BR DCH_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS ;
from inside Claude Code run it in the background. SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP
types the resume message into agents still waiting to pause and lets
restarts past
.B /exit
finish; a second signal stops that wait and reports each agent past
.B /exit
as interrupted, with its id (one already relaunched says
.B claude \-\-resume
.I id
is started in the session and the resume message was not typed: type it
there, never start a second Claude on that id; one still exiting says its
hold is left armed, which
.B dch \-\-respawn
.I name
.B \-\-cancel
disarms if Claude is still running); a third exits at once.
SIGKILL leaves a waiting agent paused.
If a second signal lands after Claude has already exited into the hold,
nothing is relaunched and the session ends when
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
runs out (default 120 s). To recover, run
.B claude \-\-resume
.I id
by hand; the id is in the
.B tail
field of
.B dch \-\-cmd
.I session
until the hold ends.
A turn that starts between the last idle check and
.B /exit
can queue the
.BR /exit ;
if that turn outlasts
.BR DCH_AGENT_STEP_MS ,
the hold is cancelled, the agent is reported as not restarted, and the
session ends when the queued
.B /exit
runs.
.IP
.B \-\-json
(with or without
.BR \-\-include\-agents )
makes stdout NDJSON: one
.RI { name ", " phase ": " master ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session from the master pass
.RI ( result " " ok ", " skipped " or " failed ),
then
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( pausing ", " exiting ", " relaunching ", " resuming )
and one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session from the agent step; no summary lines. The shapes and reason
codes are listed in the README under Machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BI \-\-respawn " name " [ \-\- " cmd..." ]
Arm a one\-shot respawn. When the session's program next exits, the master
keeps the session (socket, pty, attached clients) and starts
.I cmd
in its place. With no
.I cmd
the session is held childless until a second
.B \-\-respawn
brings one, or until
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
ends it. An unconsumed arm expires after
.BR DCH_RESPAWN_TTL_MS .
.B \-\-restart
refuses while a respawn is armed or held.
.TP
.BI \-\-respawn " name " \-\-cancel
Drop a pending respawn. A held session ends.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " name"
Print one JSON object:
.IR pid " (null while held), " state " (" running ", " armed " or " held ),
.IR argv ,
.I exit_code
and
.I exit_signal
of a child reaped under an arm (otherwise null), and
.IR tail ,
the last 8 KB the child wrote since the arm.
.TP
.BI \-\-say " name..." " " [ \-\-json ] " \-\- text..."
.TQ
.BR "\-\-say \-\-all " [ \-\-json ] " \-\- \fItext...\fP"
Type
.I text
(the words after the required
.BR \-\- ,
joined with spaces) into the Claude Code running in each named session, or
every session with
.BR \-\-all ,
and press Enter. Claude may be the session's command or typed into its
shell. Idle or busy: a busy Claude, or one running a background shell, queues
the message (after any already queued) and takes it once its turn ends.
Only a screen showing the empty composer is typed into; a prompt or dialog
(the Enter would answer it) or an unsent draft is skipped, never waited for.
Enter is pressed again while the text stays in the composer, never into a
dialog; text that left the composer, submitted or shown queued, is sent. Sessions are typed into concurrently.
One line per session as it finishes:
.IB name ": sent" ,
.IB name ": skipped \(em " why
or
.IB name ": failed \(em " why .
.B \-\-json
prints one object per line instead:
.IR name ,
.IR result " (" ok ", " skipped " or " failed ),
.I reason
.RB ( not_claude ", " self ", " prompt ", " draft ,
.BR no_screen ", " no_session ", " not_submitted ", " not_typed ,
.BR old_master ", " interrupted ", " withdrawn ", " draft_left ,
or empty) and
.IR detail .
A first SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP, once handled, types nothing more and
presses no Enter; text already in the composer is taken back out one
Ctrl\-U at a time (Claude Code's kills one wrapped row), the screen re\-read
after each. A session not yet sent fails
.B interrupted
(nothing typed),
.B withdrawn
(taken back) or
.B draft_left
(still there at the deadline, under a dialog, or a paste not painted by
the end of the land wait: it may still sit in the composer, unsent). A
second signal exits at once. Limit: an Enter sent before the signal that
Claude takes only after it sends the text, reported
.BR withdrawn .
The caller's own session
.RB ( DCH_SESSION )
is skipped. A dch-lite build has no mirror to check the composer with and
skips every Claude session
.RB ( no_screen ).
Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 on a usage error or when any session failed.
.TP
.BI \-\-stop " name..." " " [ \-\-json ]
.TQ
.BR "\-\-stop \-\-all " [ \-\-json ]
End sessions so that
.B \-\-revive
can bring them back. A session whose Claude Code is live, as its command or
typed into its shell, is paused first as
.B \-\-include\-agents
pauses it: a pause message and a wait until it is provably idle. Claude as
the command then gets
.B /exit
through the same composer gate, its resume id read from Claude's exit line
(else from its session record). Claude typed into the shell never gets
.BR /exit ,
as the shell would run whatever followed
.B claude
on its line: its resume id is read from its session record and the
session's end hangs up the shell and Claude together.
Then the session ends. Claude's session record is looked up as Claude
sees it, under its own
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
(else its
.BR HOME ),
read from the process. A session without a
live Claude ends at once; one where a process is plainly an interactive
Claude
.RB ( claude
as its argv[0]) but no record of it can be read is left running, nothing
typed:
.BR claude_unreadable .
A non\-interactive run
.RB ( "claude \-p" ,
.BR \-\-print ,
or a subcommand such as
.BR "claude mcp serve" )
has no composer to pause and counts as no Claude.
Its history record ends
.B stopped
and keeps the agent with that id;
.B \-\-revive \-\-all
skips such records and
.BI \-\-revive " name"
wakes one, Claude resumed and told it was stopped and to re\-arm what it
stopped. Sessions are handled concurrently. One line per session as it
finishes:
.IB name ": stopped (claude " "name id" "; revive with dch \-\-revive " name )
(when the session kept no record, as under
.BR DCH_NO_HISTORY ,
.IB name ": stopped (no session record kept, so dch \-\-revive cannot bring it back; resume by hand: claude \-\-resume " id ) ,
the same text as
.B \-\-json
detail),
.IB name ": skipped \(em " why
.RB ( self ", " respawn_pending ", " no_screen ),
.IB name ": failed \(em " why
.RB ( no_session ", " old_master ", " claude_unreadable ", " prompt ,
.BR draft ", " queued ", " not_typed ", " not_idle ", " exit_failed ", " claude_exited ,
.BR end_failed )
or
.IB name ": interrupted \(em " why .
A failure leaves the session running and types a resume message into its
Claude; so does a first SIGINT or SIGTERM for every Claude not yet past
.BR /exit ;
a second one reports those past
.B /exit
as interrupted with their id and leaves their marker, so the record still
ends stopped; a third exits at once.
That line, and the one for a master that stops answering after
.BR /exit ,
ends: if claude is still running, run
.B dch \-\-stop
.I name
again.
Once Claude's id is read, a line that is not ok carries it.
.B \-\-json
prints NDJSON instead:
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( pausing ", " exiting ", " ending ;
no
.B exiting
for Claude typed into the shell)
as each session reaches them, then one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session, with
.IR result " " ok ", " skipped ", " failed " or " interrupted .
A dch-lite build skips sessions with a live Claude
.RB ( no_screen );
the rest still stop. Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 on a usage error or
when any session failed or was interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-\-revive " name" "\fR|\fP" \-\-all " " [ \-\-as " new" ] " " [ \-\-no\-message ] " " [ \-\-json ]
Start ended sessions again from their history records: in their cwd, with
their argv, with their Claude Code resumed via
.B \-\-resume
.I id
(relaunched as the command, or typed into the revived shell) and told to
carry on.
Claude's startup prompt is sent once: the prompt in
.B dch \-\-spawn api \-\- claude \-\-name api \-\- \(dqfix the login bug\(dq
is its first message, and dch drops it from every later start (a revive, the
picker's resume,
.BR \-\-restart 's
agent step, a revive with no Claude id) and keeps every other option, one
written after the prompt too. The
history record stores the command without it, so
.BR \-\-history ,
.B \-\-history \-\-json
and
.B \-\-ls\-json
never show it; an older record that has one is read without it. The prompt
is everything after a bare
.BR \-\- ,
and every argument that is neither an option nor an option's value, the
options read as Claude Code 2.1.318's
.B \-\-help
lists them. It is cut only where
.B claude
is the program run: the command itself, after an
.B env
prefix, or behind a wrapper
.RB ( "headroom wrap claude" " ...)"
once dch has seen Claude run in the session.
Known limit: in such a session the first word named
.B claude
counts as the program even when it is a launcher's path argument
.RB ( "make \-C ~/src/claude dev" ,
where
.B make
starts Claude), so the words after it are cut as the prompt
.RB ( dev
here) and
.B \-\-resume
.I id
goes in right after it; start Claude directly, or through a launcher with
no word named
.BR claude .
Any other command that merely
holds a
.B claude
word
.RB ( "git \-C src/claude log" ,
.BR "docker run \-\-name claude img" ,
.BR "ssh host claude" ,
a
.B sh \-c
argument) is kept whole, and so are
.BR "npx @anthropic\-ai/claude\-code" ,
a renamed claude and
.BR claude.exe ,
which hold no word named
.BR claude .
Put it after
.BR \-\- :
an option dch does not know, followed by a word, keeps the whole command,
prompt included; an unknown
.BI \-\-no\- name
is a flag that never takes a value. Claude Code runs a one\-word prompt that names one of its
commands
.RB ( update ,
.BR doctor ,
.BR mcp ,
\&...) as that command,
.B \-\-
or not.
A Claude that ran under a config dir of its own (its
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ,
typed or in the session's environment, or another
.BR HOME )
is resumed under it: the record's agent keeps it as
.I config_dir
(empty for the default
.IR ~/.claude ),
and the relaunch gets
.BI CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR= config_dir
in its environment, or, typed into the shell, as
.B env
.BI CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR= config_dir
before the command, unless the command already sets it.
A Claude whose
.B HOME
alone differed comes back with
.IB HOME /.claude
as its
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR .
A record without
.I config_dir
(written before 1.28.2) is resumed as before.
.B \-\-no\-message
resumes it without typing that message: the result is ok and says the
resume message was not sent.
.I name
takes the newest ended record of that name, never a live session's;
.IB file .json
exactly that record file, as for
.BR \-\-pin ,
and a bare argument also matches a record file exactly as given.
A new session under a dead session's name moves that record aside to
.IB name . ended .json
first when it still holds a Claude id, so
.B \-\-history
lists it with
.RI [record " name" . ended ]
and, while the new session runs,
.B \-\-revive
.I name
.B \-\-as
.I new
brings the old one back; it is also revived, pinned and forgotten as
.IB name . ended ,
as printed, also for a long name whose moved record's file would not
survive the name shortening.
.B \-\-all
takes the sessions that died in the last 3 days after running 5 minutes or
more.
A record's end is
.BI "exit " N ,
.BI "signal " N ,
.BR killed ,
.B stopped
or
.BR vanished ;
a master that another process sent SIGTERM or SIGINT ends
.BI "signal " N " from " "pid command"
(the sender's command line made printable, cut at 120 bytes; without it
when the sender was already gone), and counts as died.
Never two Claudes on one conversation: a record whose Claude id a live
Claude already runs (probed in each live session, else found in Claude's
sessions dir or the record's
.IR config_dir )
is skipped by
.B \-\-all
and refused by name, with or without
.B \-\-as
.RB ( live_sid ,
naming the live session, or the Claude's pid outside any, and the id);
of two records with one id
.B \-\-all
revives only the newest and skips the rest
.RB ( dup_sid ).
One line per session.
.B \-\-json
prints NDJSON instead:
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( starting ", " resuming ;
no
.B resuming
with
.BR \-\-no\-message )
and one
.RI { name ", " as ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session, where
.I as
is the name it came back under.
A record stores a byte that is not UTF\-8 as U+FFFD; one whose command,
cwd or Claude launch holds U+FFFD is not revived
.RB ( not_utf8 ,
with the id to
.B claude \-\-resume
by hand), since it would run something else.
Exits 0 when nothing failed (skips included), 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-pin " name..." "\fR|\fP" \-\-unpin " name..." " " [ \-\-json ]
Keep a session's history record past the 30-day prune, or stop keeping it.
A pin is an empty
.IB file .pin
beside the record, keyed by the name: a new session under a pinned name is
pinned too, a record it moves aside keeps a pin of its own (so a pinned
name gathers pinned records until
.B \-\-unpin
.I name
releases them all), and a revive under another name moves the pin.
.B \-\-pin
and
.B \-\-unpin
act on every record of the name;
.IB file .json
(also for
.BR \-\-forget )
acts on that one record file only, the primary
.IB name .json
or a moved
.IB name . ended .json ,
never matched by name.
One that is also a session's name (sessions
.B a
and
.BR a.json )
is refused,
.BR ambiguous ,
nothing touched:
.B a.json.json
names session
.BR a.json 's
record; so is a bare argument that is one record's file and another's
name.
.B \-\-history \-\-json
gives every record its
.I file
(the record file name, without
.BR .json ).
.B \-\-pin
needs a record, live or ended
.RB ( not_found );
.B \-\-unpin
succeeds with or without a pin, but not without a record or a stray pin
.RB ( not_found ).
.TP
.BI \-\-forget " name..." " " [ \-f "] [" \-\-json ]
.TQ
.BR "\-\-forget \-\-all " [ \-\-json ]
Delete history records: every record of the name, the ones a new session
moved aside too
.RI ( name . ended .json
forgets one alone); never a live session's
.RB ( live ),
a pinned one only with
.BR \-f ,
which removes the pin too (else
.BR pinned ).
.B \-\-all
deletes every record neither live nor pinned and ends with
.BI "forgot " N " (" K " pinned, " L " live kept)" .
.B \-\-json
for
.BR \-\-pin ", " \-\-unpin " and " \-\-forget
prints one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per name (per record for
.BR \-\-all ),
then
.RI { ok ", " skipped ", " failed }
counts. Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-\-agent\-list " [" \-\-json "]"
List live authenticated Claude native peers. The default writes exact names,
one per line. With
.BR \-\-json ,
it writes an array of objects containing
.I name
and
.IR session_id .
Inside a bridged Codex session the sandbox blocks
.B ps
and signals to other processes, so liveness there means only that the
recorded pid still exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-agent\-send " peer message..."
Send a message from the current bridged Codex session to one exact peer name.
Arguments after the name are joined with spaces. The command prints
.B sent
and exits 0 once the authenticated frame has been written to the peer's
socket; it does not wait for a receipt, because Claude sends none for
messages it accepts. Ambiguous, unknown, or unreachable peers exit 1. The
source sidecar remains available after this command exits,
so the peer can reply later.
.PP
dch registers a session whose command runs
.B codex
(directly or through a wrapper such as
.BR "headroom wrap codex" )
as a Claude native peer. The peer name is the Codex thread title when the
thread has one, otherwise the
.B DCH_SESSION
name; the title is re-read every five seconds.
Registration starts after Codex writes the first shell snapshot and only when
the launch marker identifies one UUID. Multiple matching UUIDs are ambiguous;
start a fresh dch Codex session instead of risking delivery to the wrong
thread. Sessions started by older dch versions have no marker and do not
register. A native message is typed into the Codex composer through the
session master as one bracketed paste followed by Enter, so a running turn
steers it in after the current tool call. dch types only while the screen
shows the empty composer placeholder; an approval prompt, form, or pager holds
the message for up to ten minutes, a draft or an unknown screen for ten
seconds. After that, and always without the terminal mirror, delivery falls
back to
.B codex queue,
which the TUI submits at the end of the current turn. If the bound thread has
no rollout yet, the queue path retains up to 16 messages in FIFO order and
retries every five seconds. This does not start the first turn in an unused
TUI. Accepted and waiting messages produce no receipt, matching Claude's own
behaviour; only a lost message does (refused, or dropped). An orderly restart
or shutdown attempts dropped receipts for pending messages; a crash or SIGKILL
is not durable. Messages are limited to 16384 bytes and wire frames to 65536
bytes. The bridge handles one message at a time and rejects attachments.
.SH REMOTE SERVER
.B dch serve
serves this host's sessions to the Prosper iPhone app over Prosper's remote
frames protocol 1.1 on TCP port 8771, so a host with no Prosper (a Linux box,
say) shows up on the phone, and to dch's own remote client (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" ). It runs in the foreground, logs to standard error,
and exits 0 on SIGINT or SIGTERM, leaving every session running.
.PP
It listens only on this host's Tailscale IPv4, the first line of
.BR "tailscale ip \-4" .
When that reports no address it refuses to start, and it never listens on a
wildcard address. Without the
.B tailscale
CLI on
.BR PATH ,
or when
.B "tailscale status \-\-json"
fails, the owner is unknown and it refuses to start. A
peer is checked right after accept, before a byte is read, and a refused peer
is told why and closed (see Refusals below): it must be an IPv4 address in
100.64.0.0/10, not one
of this host's own addresses or its own node (any local user could connect
from those), not a tagged node, and owned by this host's user (the numeric
.B UserProfile.ID
of
.B tailscale whois \-\-json
equals
.B Self.UserID
of
.BR "tailscale status \-\-json" ).
When whois cannot answer the peer is refused; a peer refused on tailscaled's
word is refused again for 30 s without a process being started for it. The
.B tailscale
CLI is taken from an absolute
.B PATH
entry only (empty and relative ones are skipped), else from
.BR /usr/bin ,
.BR /usr/local/bin ,
.B /opt/homebrew/bin
or the Tailscale app, and logged. Log lines escape control characters
(C0, DEL, C1, bidi) from peer-supplied strings. A port already in use (Prosper's
Remote Terminal on a Mac, or another
.BR "dch serve" )
makes it exit 1; it takes no other port and does not retry.
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ipv4"
Listen on this address instead. It relaxes no peer check, so an address off
the tailnet admits nobody (a warning says so). Non-unicast addresses are
refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " n"
Another port, 0 for any. For tests: the phone dials 8771.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-create
Let the phone start sessions with its own command; without it,
.B create
is not offered, a create request is refused, and an attach reaches only a
session that is already listed.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow\-user " user"
Required on a tagged host, which no user owns: accept this user's nodes, given
as a numeric Tailscale user id (exact) or a login (ASCII case-insensitive).
Repeatable. Refused on an untagged host, which serves its owner.
.TP
.B \-\-whois\-optional
Accept a tailnet peer, with a warning, while tailscaled is not answering
(whois and
.B tailscale status
both fail). A whois that fails while status works, or answers anything that
is not clean, is still refused. The owner must still be known at startup.
.TP
.BI \-\-check\-peer " ipv4"
Print whether that peer would get in, with the refusal code in parentheses,
and exit 0 (accept) or 1 (refuse).
.PP
.B Refusals.
A tailnet peer the checks refuse, or one past the connection limits (64 in
all, 16 per peer), gets exactly one frame, sent before the
server reads a byte and before any 0x18, then the FIN:
.RS
.EX
0x13  {"code":"<code>","message":"<text>"}
.EE
.RE
.B code
is a stable contract;
.B message
is for people and may be reworded. Codes may be added; an unknown one is a
refusal that cannot be explained. A refusal never names the owner, a user, a
node or the allow list, and a repeat refusal within 30 s carries the same
code. Other 0x13 frames have no code, but for
.B taken
below. Each of these codes means refused,
do not retry:
.TP
.B not_owner
an untagged host, and the peer is another user's node:
.I this host serves only its owner's devices
.TP
.B not_allowed
a tagged host, and the peer's user is not on its
.BR \-\-allow\-user :
.I this host is tagged and serves only the users it names: start dch serve there with \-\-allow\-user <your login or id>
.TP
.B tagged_peer
the peer is a tagged node:
.I this host does not serve tagged nodes
.TP
.B whois_unavailable
tailscaled is not answering, and no
.BR \-\-whois\-optional :
.I this host could not verify you: tailscaled is not answering
.TP
.B whois_failed
whois failed while status works, or answered something unusable:
.I this host could not verify you (tailscale whois failed)
.TP
.B self
the peer is this host itself (one of its addresses, or its own node):
.I this host does not serve connections from itself
.PP
One code is transient, and means try again later (with the client's own
backoff; there is no retry\-after field):
.TP
.B busy
the host is at its connection limit (64 in all, or 16 from this peer):
.I this host is at its connection limit: try again shortly
.PP
.B busy
is never cached as a refusal, and says nothing about other users or
connections. The connection limits used to close with no frame; they now
send
.BR busy .
The frame is one non-blocking write, never retried; the server then waits
up to 1 s for the peer to close, dropping what it sent unread, so the close
is a FIN. A peer outside 100.64.0.0/10 is closed with no frame.
.PP
One code ends an attach that was let in: ended by another client, do not
retry (a retry would take the session back):
.TP
.B taken
a newer attach to the same session retired this one; the attach's last
frame, with no 0x12, then the FIN:
.I another client attached to this session
.PP
An attach request
.RB ( 0x01 ,
and
.BR 0x02 )
may carry one key beyond remote frames 1.0:
.BR \(dqscrollback\(dq: \fIN\fP ,
an integer, which reaches the
.B dch \-E \-f \-n
.I name
client it starts as
.B \-\-scrollback
.I N
(clamped to 0..10000; missing, 0, negative or not an integer is none). A
server that does not know the key ignores it, so it needed no new protocol
version.
.PP
Protocol 1.1 adds the
.B ctl
op: one of dch's control verbs, run on this host for the client. The
.B 0x18
hello says
.B \(dqproto\(dq:\(dq1.1\(dq
and always lists
.B ctl
and
.B ctl\-cancel
in
.B ops
.RB ( create
only with
.BR \-\-allow\-create );
a client checks the
.B ctl\-cancel
token, not the proto, before it sends a 0x34 or a second 0x30.
The request is
.B 0x30
.BR {\(dqargv\(dq:[...],\(dqcwd\(dq:\(dq/abs\(dq} ,
.B cwd
optional, the argv dch's own without the leading
.BR dch ;
the replies are
.B 0x31
stdout and
.B 0x32
stderr bytes as they come, then
.B 0x33
.BR {\(dqexit\(dq:\fIN\fP} ,
exactly one per request that runs. Not on an attached or watching
connection, and a ctl connection takes only hello, list, 0x30 and 0x34
(anything else: a 0x13, then the close). After the 0x33 the connection stays open for the next 0x30 (one
at a time); with no frame for 300 s it closes. A
.B 0x34
(cancel, any payload) while the verb runs ends it (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL
of its process group 2 s later; 660 s later for
.BR \-\-say ,
.BR \-\-stop ,
.B \-\-revive
and
.BR \-\-run ,
which stop at a safe point on a SIGTERM) with
.BR {\(dqexit\(dq:2,\(dqwhy\(dq:\(dqcancel\(dq} .
.B \-\-stop
types the resume message into an agent still waiting to pause;
.BR \-\-say ,
.B \-\-run
and
.B \-\-revive
type nothing more and press no Enter once the signal is handled, take
typed text back out of the composer one Ctrl\-U at a time and exit 1,
usually within seconds (up to twice the 30 s land wait when a paste is
still painting or will not come out)
.RB ( interrupted ", " withdrawn " or " draft_left ).
The dch's own exit goes to the server's log. Until the dch is reaped the
server keeps reading its pipes, dropping what it reads. A 0x34
with nothing running, or after the verb was already cut, is ignored. A
0x30 while one runs gets 0x13
.B {\(dqcode\(dq:\(dqbusy\(dq,...}
and the running one goes on; nothing closes. dch runs its own binary with
fork and exec (no shell), in
.B cwd
or else
.BR $HOME ,
stdin
.IR /dev/null ;
.I N
is its exit status, or 128 + the signal that ended it. Only these run
.RI ( n
a session name that passes the attach name check and does not start with
.BR \- ):
.RS
.nf
\-\-read \fIn\fP [\-\-ansi] [\-\-recent [\fIN\fP]] [\-\-cursor]
\-\-send|\-\-run \fIn\fP [\-\-] \fItext...\fP
\-\-keys \fIn\fP \fIkey...\fP
\-\-follow \fIn\fP
\-\-status \fIn\fP
\-k \fIn\fP;  \-d \fIn\fP;  \-m \fIn\fP \fIalias\fP
\-\-spawn \fIn\fP [\-\-size \fIC\fPx\fIR\fP] [\-\-] \fIcmd...\fP    (create, and cwd)
\-\-history \-\-json
\-\-revive \fIn\fP [\-\-as \fInew\fP] [\-\-no\-message] \-\-json    (create)
\-\-pin|\-\-unpin|\-\-forget \fIn\fP... \-\-json;  \-\-forget \-\-all \-\-json
\-\-stop \fIn\fP... \-\-json
\-\-say \fIn\fP \-\-json \-\- \fItext\fP
.fi
.RE
.PP
Anything else is refused with an uncoded 0x13, then the FIN, and nothing
runs: an argv of
fewer than 2 elements, a verb or flag off that list, a bad name, a name
with
.B :
.RI ( "a session name with \(aq:\(aq is local only" :
the host's dch would read it as host:name and dial on with the host's
identity),
.BR \-\-config ,
.BR serve ,
.B \-h
or
.B \-f
outside the free text,
.B \-\-all
but in
.BR "\-\-forget \-\-all \-\-json" ,
a
.B cwd
that is no directory
.RI ( "no such directory" ),
and a (create) verb without
.B \-\-allow\-create
.RI ( "creating sessions is off on this host (dch serve \-\-allow\-create)" ).
.B \-\-xray
is reserved for a later release:
.IR "xray not supported by this host" .
Stdout and stderr together get 8 MiB; at that cap the server stops
forwarding, sends the dch SIGTERM (SIGKILL 2 s later) and ends with
.BR {\(dqexit\(dq:2,\(dqwhy\(dq:\(dqcap\(dq} .
The timeout is 660 s for
.BR \-\-say ,
.BR \-\-stop ,
.B \-\-revive
and
.BR \-\-run ,
none for
.BR \-\-follow ,
60 s for the rest; past it, the same with
.BR \(dqwhy\(dq:\(dqtimeout\(dq .
A client that closes the connection while its verb runs ends it as a 0x34
does (the same 2 s or 660 s grace), with no 0x33 (one that sent a 0x34
first still gets it); the handler holds its connection slot, one of the
peer's 16, until that dch is gone (on purpose: it is that peer's dch still
running), and a server stop cuts the grace to 2 s. The gate is
attach's. For tests,
.B DCH_CTL_TIMEOUT_MS
replaces every timeout
.RB ( \-\-follow 's
too),
.B DCH_CTL_CAP
lowers the cap and
.B DCH_CTL_IDLE_MS
the warm connection's 300 s; all are read at startup and removed from the
environment.
.PP
.B DCH_SERVE_TEST_LOOPBACK=1
with a loopback
.B \-\-bind
lets loopback peers past the address check. It exists for the test suite only,
and is removed from the environment at startup.
.SH REMOTE HOSTS
dch lists and attaches to the sessions of other machines that serve them
over Prosper's remote frames on TCP port 8771
.RB ( "dch serve" ,
or Prosper on a Mac).
The hosts are
.BR \-\-hosts ,
else the
.B remote_hosts
key, else discovery: the peers in
.B tailscale status \-\-json
that are online, owned by this host's Tailscale user (numeric user id) and
untagged, each at its 100.64.0.0/10 address, named by the first label of its
MagicDNS name, and only while tailscaled reports BackendState Running.
Nothing else is ever dialled; no
.B tailscale
CLI or no peers means no remote rows, not an error.
Before an attach to a discovered host,
.B tailscale whois
on its address must name this host's user and no tags, or dch does not
connect; a host given in
.B \-\-hosts
or
.B remote_hosts
is taken as given.
.PP
.BR \-ls ,
.B \-lj
and
.B \-\-ls\-json
list remote rows only with
.BR \-\-remote ,
which probes every host in parallel within 300 ms in all, the
.B tailscale
call and name lookups included. A host that does
not answer in time shows its last answer, stale, for 2 minutes (a cache in
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/dch/sessions/.remote\-cache ).
A host speaking a newer protocol major is skipped with a note. A host whose
server refuses us (a 0x13 with a code) is said on standard error as
.IR "dch: host refused: message" ,
and none of its cached rows are shown;
.B \-a
to it prints the same and exits 4. A
.B busy
host is not a refusal but a host that is down: its cached rows show stale,
and
.B \-a
prints
.I "dch: host: says: message"
and exits 1.
The picker lists remote rows in Sessions, the host faint, of the hosts that
are candidates now, and re\-probes in the background; Enter attaches, on
the connection that checked the name is listed, and no other action applies
to them yet.
.PP
.B dch \-\-history \-\-remote
.RB ( \-\-json
too) prints the local records unchanged, then each host's, fetched as its
.B dch \-\-history \-\-json
over the
.B ctl
op within the same 300 ms box: text lines as
\fIhost\fP:\fIname\fP ..., JSON records as the host sent them (C1, DEL
and bidi controls as \fI\\uXXXX\fP) plus
.I host
and
.IR stale .
A record with a
.I host
or
.I stale
key of its own, or no
.IR name ,
is dropped; at most 500 records and 2 MiB per host. A host that does not
answer in time shows its last records, stale
.RB ( [stale] ),
for 2 minutes (a cache in
.IR .remote\-history );
the picker's background probe refreshes it every 10 s. A host whose hello
lacks
.B ctl
prints
.I "dch: host: does not support remote history (needs dch serve 1.41+)"
and adds nothing; a refusal prints
.IR "dch: host refused: message" .
Neither changes the exit status or the local records.
.PP
An attach is a local one over the network: the detach character and its
double press stay local, the window size follows the terminal, and a detach
closes the connection and leaves the remote session running. A lost
connection restores the terminal, prints why and exits 1. An attach a newer
attach to the same session took over (the 0x13
.BR taken )
restores the terminal, prints
.I "dch: host: another client attached to this session"
and exits 5.
.PP
The control verbs that
.B dch serve
runs (see
.BR "REMOTE SERVER" )
take
.IR host : name
too: such a name is remote only when no local session or History record
has exactly that name and
.I host
is a known host
.RB ( \-\-hosts ,
.BR remote_hosts ,
or a cached remote row), never with remote hosts off
.RB ( DCH_NO_REMOTE=1 ,
.BR "remote_hosts = off" );
anything else stays local, so a local session named
.I proj:task
can be spawned, revived and forgotten by name. When such a name fails
locally while remote hosts are on, one more line on standard error names the
host:
.RI ( "host is not a known host; \-\-host host to reach it" );
the exit status is the local one. It comes when the verb returns its
failure; one that exits on the spot prints none, and with remote hosts off
none comes at all.
.B \-\-host
is the unambiguous form (the one for a host only discovery knows, and the
only one for
.BR \-\-history ),
and a discovered host must pass the same whois check. Names on two hosts in
one command are refused. dch runs the verb there and writes its standard
output and error as they come, byte for byte, and exits with its exit
status. A form the host would refuse is refused here before any connection
.RI ( "dch: can't run on a remote host: why" ,
exit 1). A host whose hello lacks
.B ctl
(or
.B create
for
.B \-\-spawn
and
.BR \-\-revive )
prints
.I "dch: host: does not support verb (needs dch serve 1.41+)"
.RI ( "(needs dch serve \-\-allow\-create)" )
and exits 6; one that cannot be reached, or whose connection is lost before
the exit status, prints
.I "dch: host: unreachable: why"
and exits 7. The host's own refusal of the request prints
.I "dch: host: why"
and exits 1; a refusal by its gate exits 4. A verb the host cut short exits
2 and prints
.IR "dch: host: verb stopped by the host (cap|timeout)" .
SIGINT (Ctrl-C), SIGTERM or a closed standard output cancel the verb on the
host: a 0x34 when its hello lists
.BR ctl\-cancel ,
then up to 3 s for its 0x33 (else the close, which ends it there too). dch
then dies of the signal, or exits 0 for a closed output. A second signal
ends it at once.
.PP
Every name and message a peer sends is printed with control characters
escaped as \fI\\xNN\fP
.RB ( \-\-ls\-json
writes C1 and bidi controls as \fI\\uXXXX\fP);
only an attach's screen bytes pass as they are.
.PP
.B dch.exe
(built by
.BR scripts/build\-windows.sh )
is this client alone for Windows 10 or later: it hosts no sessions, so
.B \-ls
implies
.BR \-\-remote ,
.B \-a
takes
.IR host : name ,
the control verbs take
.IR host : name
or
.B \-\-host
(with
.BR \-\-host ,
.B \-\-hosts
and
.B \-\-cwd
before the verb), and
.B dch
alone is the picker with the remote rows only (no History, Sidekicks,
row actions or settings).
Its config is
.IR %APPDATA%\(rsdch\(rsconfig ,
its cache is in
.IR %LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsdch ,
and its time box defaults to 1000 ms.
.SH CONFIG FILE
Optional: dch needs none. Read fresh on every use from
.BR $DCH_CONFIG ,
else
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dch/config ,
else
.IR ~/.config/dch/config :
.I key
=
.I value
lines,
.B #
comments, the last line for a key wins.
.TP
.B dch \-\-config
Print the path, then every known key as
.IR "key = value" ,
its effective value.
.TP
.BI "dch \-\-config " key
Print the key's value; exit 1 when it is unset.
.TP
.BI "dch \-\-config " "key value"
Set it: the file is rewritten whole (a temp file renamed over it), every other
line and comment kept, the parent directory created. An unknown key, a
boolean key given anything but true/false/1/0/yes/no/on/off, or a choice key
given a value not its own, exits 1. The picker's settings view writes the same
way.
.TP
.BI "dch \-\-config " key " \-\-unset"
Remove it.
.TP
.B auto_allow
Boolean, default false. When true, the session's master answers Claude Code's
plain permission prompt itself by typing
.B 1
(the preselected Yes; never Enter): exactly "Do you want to proceed?" over a
bare "Yes", at most one "Yes, and don't ask again ..." row (a curly apostrophe
counts; never picked, never a mode change), and a last "No...", the dialog the last thing on the screen
and the screen reading
.BR blocked .
Everything else is left to the human, and hook- or ask-rule-raised
prompts ("Hook ... requires confirmation for this command", "Permission rule
... requires confirmation for this ...", "Ask rule ... overrides auto mode for
this ...") always are. Checked after 300 ms of quiet output and
2 s after an answer, only when a Claude Code runs under the session's program;
a prompt it answered is never answered again while it stays up, nor within
30 s once gone and back (its ticking "will automatically block this request in
M:SS" line aside). Needs the terminal mirror: unavailable in dch\-lite
and under
.BR DCH_NO_VT .
.TP
.B sidekick_pattern
A string read by the session picker, default
.BR \-qa ;
empty: no Sidekicks tab.
.TP
.B picker_sort
.B activity
(default) or
.BR name :
the session picker's sort, also set by its
.B s
key.
.TP
.B detach_key
.B detach
(default) or
.BR switch :
what one press of the detach character does; two presses do the other (see
.BR "SWITCHING SESSIONS" ).
.TP
.B remote_hosts
Empty (default): the remote hosts are your tailnet peers;
.BR off :
none; else a
.B \-\-hosts
list (see
.BR "REMOTE HOSTS" ).
.TP
.B focus_resize
.B true
(default) or
.BR false :
whether an attached client takes the session's size when its window gains
focus (see
.BR "FOCUS AND SIZE" ).
.TP
.B xray_ring
.B 0
(off) to
.BR 16M ,
default
.BR 2M :
the shell output each new session keeps in its master's memory for X\-ray
(the rows that scroll off its primary screen, as text, with OSC 133 prompt
marks). Kept across
.B \-\-restart
(a restart asked by an older dch, which may predate the ring, starts empty
and gets the core\-dump limit back);
never on disk, never in core dumps.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Control verbs: 1 generic error, 2
.B \-\-wait
timeout, 3 unavailable in this build. A dch-lite build has no terminal mirror;
native agent commands are available in both builds.
.PP
.B dch \-a
.I host:name
(and
.BR dch.exe ):
0 detached or the session ended; 1 the connection was lost, the host is
down or
.BR busy ,
or another error (a lost link or busy may be retried); 4 refused by the
host's gate (do not retry); 5 taken by another client's attach (do not
retry).
.PP
A control verb on
.I host:name
(or with
.BR \-\-host ):
0\-3 the remote dch's own exit status; also 1 the host refused the request,
an unknown host or a request dch would not send; 4 refused by the host's
gate; 6 the host's
.B dch serve
lacks the op (1.41+ or
.BR \-\-allow\-create );
7 the host is unreachable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DCH_CONFIG
Path of the config file (see
.BR "CONFIG FILE" ).
.TP
.B DCH_SOCKET_DIR
Session socket directory (default
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dch\-$UID ,
falling back to
.I /tmp/dch\-$UID
when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset \(em the common case on macOS). For a session meant to sit idle for
days, point this somewhere other than
.I /tmp
so a periodic cleaner never has a reason to touch it;
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
itself is honored the same way when set.
.TP
.B DCH_FOCUS_RESIZE
Read by the attaching client.
.BR 0 ", " false ", " no " or " off
turns off focus reporting and size on focus\-in (see
.BR "FOCUS AND SIZE" );
any other value turns it on. Unset: the
.B focus_resize
config key decides, default on.
.TP
.B DCH_FOLLOW_MS
Least time between two
.B \-\-follow
frames, in milliseconds (default 150, clamped to 20\(en10000).
.TP
.B DCH_HEAL_MS
Self\-heal tick interval in milliseconds (default 3600000, i.e. hourly); see
.B DESCRIPTION
above. Mainly a test knob for shortening the interval.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_VT
Empty or 0 is off; anything else, when a session is spawned, disables
its terminal mirror.
.TP
.B DCH_SCROLLBACK
Mirror scrollback lines (default 2000, max 100000), read when the session is
spawned. At least that many lines of history are kept above the screen, and
at most about one page more (606 lines at 80 columns, 244 at 200): history is
dropped a whole page at a time, and a page holds about 48000/(columns+1)
lines. 0 keeps no scrollback, only the visible screen. Memory grows with
depth and width: 100000 lines take about 70 MB at 80 columns and 170 MB
at 200.
.B \-\-read \-\-recent
reads at most 65535 lines of it.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_REPLAY
Empty or 0 is off; anything else, when a session is spawned, does not replay the mirrored screen to
attaching clients. Attach then behaves as it did before: the client's screen
is cleared and the program is asked to repaint itself, which diff\-based
renderers do not do. Escape hatch only.
.TP
.B DCH_REPLAY_SCROLLBACK
Read by the attaching client: the lines of scrollback the attach replay
prints above the screen, as
.B \-\-scrollback
.I N
(which wins), max 10000, default 0. Sent to a remote host too.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_DETECT
Empty or 0 is off; anything else turns off screen\-content state detection
(agents read 0) and the master's auto\-allow.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_HISTORY
Empty or 0 is off; anything else, when a session is spawned, keeps no
history record for it.
.TP
.B DCH_REMOTE_BOX_MS
The time box, in ms, for probing every remote host in the foreground
(default 300, max 60000), for a slow link.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_REMOTE
Empty or 0 is off; anything else lists and discovers no remote hosts in
the picker,
.B \-a
or
.BR \-\-remote ,
and
.BR \-a 's
or a control verb's
.I host:name
stays local
.RB ( \-\-hosts
still applies).
.TP
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
Read by the master: how long a held (childless) session waits for its
next command before ending (default 120000).
.TP
.B DCH_RESPAWN_TTL_MS
Read by the master: how long an unconsumed respawn arm lives (default 300000).
.TP
.B DCH_PAUSE_MSG
Text
.B "\-\-restart \-\-include\-agents"
or
.B \-\-stop
types to pause a Claude agent (default begins
.I "pause all work, i need to restart you"
or
.IR "pause all work, this session is being stopped" ).
.TP
.B DCH_RESUME_MSG
Text typed into a relaunched Claude agent, or one
.B \-\-stop
gives up on (default begins
.IR "resume all work" ).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS
How long an agent may take to pause (default 600000), for
.B \-\-include\-agents
and
.BR \-\-stop .
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_SETTLE_MS
How long an agent must stay ready before
.B /exit
(default 3000).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_SHELL_MS
How long the agent's record may read
.B shell
after the pause before the agent counts as idle (default 60000).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_LAND_MS
How long dch waits for typed text to show in the composer before giving up
without pressing Enter (default 30000); the wait ends as soon as it shows.
.TP
.B DCH_LOGIN_ENV
.B 0
stops
.B \-\-revive
from taking the environment of the login shell
.RB ( "$SHELL \-l \-i" ,
all variables except
.BR DCH_* ),
which it otherwise does so that a revive started from a GUI app gets the
.B PATH
the rc files set up.
.TP
.B DCH_LOGIN_ENV_MS
How long that login shell may take before dch keeps its own environment
(default 10000).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_STEP_MS
How long
.B /exit
may take to end Claude, and the relaunch to show its composer (default
60000).
.TP
.B DCH_DEBUG
Trace to a file (path, or 1 for
.IR /tmp/dch.<uid>.trace ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dtach (1),
.BR screen (1),
.BR tmux (1)
